# Google Drive credentials — never commit
config/credentials.json
config/token.json
config/drive.yaml

# Service-account keys. The key belongs OUTSIDE the repo, referenced by path
# through $NP_DRIVE_SA_KEY and chmod 600 — never copied in here. These patterns
# exist because one was found at the repo root, mode 0644 and unignored, where
# `git add -A` would have committed it. A key that cannot be staged cannot be
# leaked by a careless commit; that is the whole job of this block.
/*.json
np-autopilot-*.json
*service-account*.json
*service_account*.json
*-sa-key*.json
gcp-*.json

# THE ONE EXCEPTION to `/*.json`, and it needs stating because the rule above is
# there for a real reason: a live service-account key was once sitting at the
# repo root, mode 0644, unignored.
#
# But Vercel reads its configuration from the REPOSITORY. An ignored vercel.json
# does not fail to deploy — it deploys WITHOUT the security headers: no CSP, no
# HSTS, no frame-options, and the page's inline scripts run because nothing is
# there to refuse them. The site comes up, looks correct, and is unprotected.
# That is the A.7b shape in a config file, so it gets a test as well as a
# comment: tests/test_login_flow.py::test_vercel_config_is_actually_tracked.
!/vercel.json

# Environment files. Supabase connection strings and the service-role key are
# secrets; they belong in a file outside the repo (mode 600), named by path.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The sensitive half of the graph. NEVER COMMITTED.
# 277 hiring rejections and 1,625 in-pipeline candidates — named external people.
# Under the Q3 two-role decision this is `recruiting` data, and a personal repo
# has no read-only tier, so committing it grants every collaborator access to it
# permanently, including through history. DECISIONS §F.2.
knowledge/graph-sensitive.json

# Build intermediates that carry the SAME funnel data graph-sensitive.json was
# split out to withhold — and more of it raw. resolved.json holds all 1,625
# in-pipeline and 277 rejected instructor nodes; candidates.json holds 1,895 and
# 369 unresolved rows of the same. Splitting graph.json while committing these
# would have been security theatre.
#
# Neither is needed at plugin runtime: the commands read graph.json through
# query.py. Both are regenerated by passes 2 and 3 on any machine with the
# corpus. Untracking them also removes ~20 MB from the repo.
knowledge/candidates.json
knowledge/resolved.json

# The extraction rejection log. Same reasoning, found during the history rewrite:
# historical versions named 140 withheld people and cited
# `SME Tracker - Bullseye_IK.xlsx!Tracker - All roles` WITH A ROW NUMBER, which
# discloses that a named external person sits in the SME hiring funnel. The
# current version happens to carry none — the six name-shape filters that
# rejected them were converted to flags — but "happens to carry none today" is
# not a property worth committing against.
#
# Nothing reads it: 02_extract.py writes it and no consumer opens it.
knowledge/rejections.json

# Local corpus cache, fetched by pipeline/00_fetch_drive.py
.drive-cache/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CORPUS IS NEVER COMMITTED.
# 75 source files holding ~8,000 learner names and personal emails, employee
# performance records, and a payroll export with termination records. The plugin
# does not bundle the corpus (doc 08 Q7) — teammates get knowledge/ only.
# Committing these would recreate exactly the exposure documented in doc 04.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
00-master/
01-workflows/
02-curriculum/
03-instructors/
04-programs/
05-operations/
06-analysis/

# Local corpus cache from pass 0
.drive-cache/

# Curation exports are REGENERATED output, not source. config/*.yaml is what a
# human edits and what the pipeline reads; an exported file sitting beside it in
# the tree is an invitation to edit the wrong one.
config/*.exported.yaml
config/*.rt-check.yaml
